{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested: one P2 availability defect at exact head 8a2b82da31414dde799ec7447a688c7390584585. I reviewed all six delivered paths, including the complete plan and report, relative to approved main a319360aed3475e6c5b1534fd37f78e5790a47a8. Four existing paths change, two plans are added, and 593 other tracked files are byte-identical to that approved main. The implementation is bound to git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7da437bcb901741837f61bc149a00c723c7c8217, with the complete plan 003 conditions inherited from git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:4efb1ef348723189487e0a383b25db5b71d661f7; the committed plan's SHA256 is 731c4ceef627139bb881b7f43c65339028494e449a4f489f7652f627ed07e5ea.\n\nP2 — Keep ended-token cleanup off the publication serial queue. packages/git/src/landing/engine.ts@8a2b82da31414dde799ec7447a688c7390584585:340-351 awaits each old token's revoke, and line 397 awaits that entire routine inside reconcile's serial callback. publish and later reconcile calls use the same chain (lines 230-243). Once an old revocation has been sent but has not answered, an independently prepared and reserved later publication cannot even start its push; its slot is now held behind cleanup for an already-landed operation. A slow batch can similarly delay subsequent preparation and publication. The durable cleanup record is independent, but its driver still has this global dependency. The current tests cover rejected revocations, which return control, and do not cover an unanswered one.\n\nIndependent reproduction, using the actual Landing engine, real local Git and SQLite: land A with revoke failing; recover other provider calls; hold only tok_1's later revocation on a deferred gate; advance to A's due time and start reconcile; accept, prepare and reserve B; call publish. While the old gate is unanswered, B's push count never increases (only A's push exists), B has no receipt and its state/slot is publishing/held. Releasing only the old gate lets B push and land exactly once. The named diagnostic 'checker: a pending ended-token revocation must not block a later publication' is red on this exact engine (1 push versus 2 expected), and green when only engine.ts is replaced with the parent a319 version while retaining the new core and the same fixture. This checks a dependency, not a requested millisecond SLA. Evidence and standalone fixture: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-diagnostics/packages/git/test/checker-terminal-pending.test.ts; /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-pending-exact-final.log; /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-pending-parent-engine-final.log.\n\nRepair within this same implementation request/promise: drive known terminal revocation without awaiting it on the publication chain. Retain durable ownership, bounded scheduling/backoff and stopped-instance fencing, prevent uncontrolled duplicate attempts, and preserve the requirement that held publication recovery runs first. Keep later legitimate publication able to progress while an old revocation is pending. Do not drop debt, infer a publication outcome from expiry/revocation, or sweep unrelated canonical tokens. Add the pending-answer regression and a restart/late-answer control to the delivered suite, update the report, and republish all changed paths at one exact head. No new outcome, authority, performer or target is requested.\n\nArchitecture: this changes lane B's Git landing core/driver and the Room lane A alarm dependency, within application-side execution over ordinary Git storage (the Gitseq architecture's storage/application boundary). It changes no Gitseq kernel or Workroom fold contract. Artroom's public LandOp/receipts, Git CAS, token TTL, complete-forward semantics, slot ordering and signed admission contract are preserved by the intended design; no public protocol amendment is needed for the private cleanup table. Per-token durable duties and transactional completion are a sound fit, but the queue dependency above fails plan 003's independent cleanup and unchanged later-publication behavior.\n\nSecurity: examined known token attribution, provider failures and late answers, durable transactions, restart adoption, expiry, secret-free public views and unrelated canonical tokens. No canonical inventory sweep, new credential authority or public token disclosure is introduced. Unknown mints remain with git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:10fcfe4eb92e946549c20b66da2a915f984db91e and are not falsely settled here. The unresolved availability defect prevents approval. Cleanup attention/projection remains separate.\n\nSimplification: the private table, one-time legacy adoption and per-token capped backoff satisfy the existing requirement with a small change. Preserve that separation and remove the cleanup dependency on the global publication chain; do not add a second publication outcome machine or unrelated feature cuts.\n\nFresh independent validation in clean /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v1 at the exact head: root npm run typecheck exit 0 (including the Git package); root npm test exit 0, 1,944 passed and 1 skipped; Git test:workers exit 0, 9 passed; node --test packages/git/test/landing.test.ts exit 0, all 45 passed. The initial dependency-less setup runs were discarded and are not candidate evidence. Root logs: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v1-types-ready.log, /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v1-root-ready.log, /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-v1-workers-ready.log. git diff --check passes and tracked checkout is clean.\n\nI independently broke all 20 reported guards in a separate diagnostic checkout: every runtime-valid mutant produced at least one named regression failure, then both source files were restored. Results: /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-mutants.json and individual M1-M20 logs. Additional 25-token control: 20 processed, five remain due, second reconcile clears the rest, unique receipt and free slot preserved; changing the batch bound to 40 makes that named control red. Separate real workerd/DO SQLite control passes across actual restarts: failure inserting the duty rolls terminal state, receipt and slot release back atomically; failure deleting the duty after a remote revocation retains the debt, rolls back the durable revoked flag and persists backoff; recovery clears it without a duplicate receipt. Its exact-source replay passes in /tmp/artroom-checker-cc-terminal-sql-exact.log. These diagnostics add no production change. No deployment, remote mutation, live credentials or inference was performed. Passing ordinary gates and these controls do not resolve the pending-answer defect.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:46422ac44e22e9af43d22aeb429be4dd43e6b4cb","binding":"assigned","head":"8a2b82da31414dde799ec7447a688c7390584585","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16addc618fedf53e01859cc0d33b75296d324b07\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f38146ead8de8da66cef9454a4e77788915624e6\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:06c4a204b10e6e8883c3dbdd2ef9a38ab24b6b71","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}